<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The languages the app ships. Declaring them puts SapGlance in Android 13+'s per-app language
    picker (Settings > Apps > SapGlance > Language), which matters for two reasons beyond
    politeness: a bilingual reader can have a Russian widget on an English phone, and anyone
    checking the Russian catalog can do it without switching the whole device over and back.

    Below API 33 this is ignored and the system language decides, which is the same thing the
    catalog does anyway — AppContainer reads Locale.getDefault().language once per process.

    Keep this in step with TipCatalog.SUPPORTED_LANGUAGES and the TipLanguage enum. A test pins
    those last two against each other; nothing can pin this file to either, because it is Android
    resources and they are plain Kotlin in a module with no Android on its classpath. Listing a
    language here that the catalog does not have would offer the reader a translation that
    silently falls back to English tips.

    Note that this only drives the *system* picker. SapGlance also has its own toggle in Settings
    (TipLanguage), which is what a reader on API 26-32 gets, and which is the one that decides
    which catalog the widget reads.
-->
<locale-config xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <locale android:name="en" />
    <locale android:name="ru" />
</locale-config>
